<Layout
    title="Freelancing Tax in Pakistan 2026-27: Rates, PSEB & Filing"
    description="Freelancing tax in Pakistan explained: 0.25% PSEB rate vs 1%, how tax is deducted, PSEB registration, filing steps and a freelancer tax calculator guide."
>
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <header class="mb-8">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-2">
                Published: October 23, 2026 · Last Updated: October 24, 2026 ·
                By <a href="/about/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                    >Khuram Shahzad</a
                >
            </p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">
                Freelancing Tax in Pakistan 2026-27: Rates, PSEB & Filing
            </h1>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600">
                If you earn from Upwork, Fiverr, YouTube, direct foreign
                clients, or a remote job paid in dollars, you probably want one
                answer: how much tax do I actually owe?
            </p>
        </header>

        <section class="space-y-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
                For most IT and IT-enabled freelancers receiving money from
                abroad, the answer is small. It is often 0.25% of what lands in
                your bank account, deducted automatically. But you only get that
                rate, and only keep it as your final tax, if you meet a few
                conditions.
            </p>
            <p>
                This guide covers who pays, how much, how PSEB registration fits
                in, what happens with local clients, and how to file without
                losing the concession.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Last updated:</strong> October 2026 (covers Finance Act 2026,
                effective 1 July 2026)
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Quick Answer: Do Freelancers Pay Tax in Pakistan?
            </h2>
            <p>
                Yes. Freelance income is not tax-free in Pakistan. Foreign
                freelance income is taxed through a special export regime, which
                is much lighter than normal income tax slabs.
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    <strong
                        >PSEB-registered IT freelancer, on the Active Taxpayer
                        List (ATL):</strong
                    > 0.25% on export proceeds.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Registered filer without PSEB:</strong> 1%.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Non-filers:</strong> higher deductions — confirm with
                    your bank.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Local clients:</strong> a separate system, taxed under
                    normal income tax rules.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                The Finance Act 2026 extended the 0.25% concession through tax
                year 2029, so the rate is stable for now.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Why the rate is low:</strong> the state wants foreign currency
                to flow through banks. Your "tax" is really the price of keeping your
                income documented. Freelancers who skip the paperwork pay far more
                than those who comply.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Freelancer Tax Rates in Pakistan for 2026-27
            </h2>
            <p>
                These are the rates under Section 154A of the Income Tax
                Ordinance, 2001, which covers export of IT and IT-enabled
                services:
            </p>

            <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
                    <thead class="bg-gray-50">
                        <tr>
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Your status</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Rate on export proceeds</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Treatment</th
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >PSEB-registered, filer (on ATL)</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"><strong>0.25%</strong></td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Final tax, subject to conditions</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Not PSEB-registered, filer (on ATL)</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"><strong>1%</strong></td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Final tax, subject to conditions</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >PSEB-registered, non-filer</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Higher — confirm with your bank</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Usually not a safe final-tax position</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3">Not registered, non-filer</td>
                            <td class="p-3">Higher — confirm with your bank</td>
                            <td class="p-3"
                                >Usually not a safe final-tax position</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-4">
                <strong>A note on non-filer rates:</strong> published sources disagree.
                Some show doubled rates for non-filers, while others say the service-export
                rate stays the same for everyone. Confirm with your bank before relying
                on any figure. Don't plan your finances around being a non-filer anyway.
                Getting on the ATL costs far less than the extra deductions you would
                risk.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                How Freelancer Tax Is Calculated (With Examples)
            </h2>
            <p>The formula is simple:</p>
            <p>
                <strong>Tax = Export income in PKR × Tax rate</strong>
            </p>
            <p>
                The bank usually converts your foreign payment to rupees at the
                rate on the day it is credited, then deducts the tax. Business
                expenses do not reduce this tax. The low rate is the trade-off
                for losing deductions.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Example 1: Mid-level developer
            </h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
