            <p class="mt-4">
                <strong>What about platform fees?</strong> Platforms like Upwork take
                their fee before paying you. The bank only sees what reaches your
                account, and the withholding applies to that credited amount. Some
                calculators apply the rate to gross income instead, so treat a calculator
                as an estimate and confirm with your bank. In either case, you cannot
                claim laptop, internet or rent expenses against this tax.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Which Tax Bucket Does Your Income Fall Into?
            </h2>
            <p>
                Not all freelance income gets the 0.25% rate. Every rupee falls
                into one of three buckets:
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Bucket 1: Foreign clients, IT or IT-enabled services
            </h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Examples: web and app development, software, graphic design,
                    UI/UX, SEO, digital marketing, virtual assistance, data
                    entry, customer support, video editing.
                </li>
                <li>Paid in foreign currency through a Pakistani bank.</li>
                <li><strong>Rate:</strong> 0.25% (PSEB) or 1%.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Bucket 2: Foreign clients, other services
            </h3>
            <p>
                Non-IT services such as consulting or advisory work provided to
                foreign clients generally fall under the 1% export rate or the
                normal business-income rules, depending on the facts. A tax
                adviser can confirm where your work sits.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Bucket 3: Pakistani clients
            </h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Section 154A does not apply to local clients.</li>
                <li>
                    Local clients may withhold tax before paying you, and the
                    rate depends on your filer status.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The income goes into your return and is taxed under normal
                    slabs.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                If you also hold a salaried job, estimate that part with our{
                    " "
                }
                <a
                    href="/blog/salary-tax-calculator-pakistan/"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                >
                    salary tax calculator
                </a>, then add your local freelance income on top.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Tip for mixed earners:</strong> keep clean records of foreign
                and local income. If they blur together, the foreign income can end
                up in the wrong tab of your return and be taxed at the higher slab
                rates.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                PSEB Freelancer Registration: Fee, Benefits and Steps
            </h2>
            <p>
                The Pakistan Software Export Board (PSEB) is the government body
                that registers IT exporters. For freelancers, its certificate is
                the key to the 0.25% rate.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                PSEB freelancer registration benefits
            </h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    <strong>A lower tax rate:</strong> 0.25% instead of 1%, which
                    is 75% less tax.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Credibility:</strong> a verifiable registration number
                    you can show clients, banks and visa officers.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Access to PSEB programs:</strong> government-backed IT
                    export initiatives.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Cleaner paperwork:</strong> your bank can tag inward remittances
                    correctly for the concession.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                PSEB freelancer registration fee
            </h3>
            <p>
                Sources quote different figures. The fee varies by category and
                is updated periodically. Check the current fee on the PSEB
                portal (pseb.org.pk) before you pay. Whatever it is, the fee is
                small next to the saving. At Rs 3 million in annual export
                income, you save Rs 22,500.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Do you have to renew?
            </h3>
            <p>
                Yes. Registration must be renewed annually. A lapsed certificate
                can mean your bank deducts 1% instead of 0.25%.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Is PSEB registration worth it?
            </h3>
            <p>
                If you earn more than a few hundred thousand rupees a year from
                IT exports, yes. The registration fee is recovered almost
                immediately.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Conditions for Keeping the 0.25% Final Tax
            </h2>
            <p>
                The tax your bank deducts is your final tax only if you meet the
                Section 154A conditions. In practice:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Your income is from IT or IT-enabled services provided to
                    foreign clients.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Your PSEB registration is active at the time of each
                    payment.
                </li>
                <li>
                    You are on the Active Taxpayer List (ATL). Filing your
                    return each year keeps you there.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Payment arrives through a bank in Pakistan in foreign
                    currency, not via crypto, hawala or informal channels.
                </li>
                <li>
                    You file your annual return by the deadline and declare the
                    income in the correct section.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                If you miss these, the deduction may not be treated as final
                tax. In that case, the income can be pulled back into normal
                slab taxation.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Think of it as a deal:</strong> the government accepts 0.25%,
                and in return you route income through banks and keep your filing
                current. Break either side of the deal and the concession is at risk.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                How to Register as a Freelancer in Pakistan, Step by Step
            </h2>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Step 1: Get your NTN
            </h3>
            <p>
                Register on the FBR's IRIS portal (iris.fbr.gov.pk) with your
                CNIC and mobile number. Choose a business activity that matches
                your work, such as IT services or software development.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Step 2: Register with PSEB
            </h3>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Go to the PSEB portal and choose freelancer registration.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Upload your CNIC, NTN, and proof of foreign income (such as
                    an Upwork, Fiverr or Payoneer statement or a client
                    invoice).
                </li>
                <li>Pay the registration fee.</li>
                <li>Download your certificate and note its renewal date.</li>
            </ol>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Step 3: Set up your bank properly
            </h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Open an account that can receive foreign remittances.</li>
                <li>Give the bank your PSEB certificate and NTN.</li>
                <li>
                    Ask it to apply the 0.25% rate and confirm in writing how it
                    will tag your inward remittances.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Step 4: Keep your proof
            </h3>
            <p>
                Every foreign payment should come with an inward remittance
                advice (often called a PRC or FIRC). Keep these with the
                matching invoices or platform statements.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Step 5: Get on the ATL and file
            </h3>
            <p>
                Once registered, file your return each year so you stay on the
                Active Taxpayer List.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Filing Your Freelancer Income Tax Return
            </h2>
            <p>
                <strong>Do you have to file if tax is already deducted?</strong>
                Yes. The deduction does not replace the return. Filing is what keeps
                you on the ATL and protects the final-tax treatment.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">When to file</h3>
            <p>
                The annual return is normally due on 30 September after the tax
                year ends. For income earned in FY 2026-27 (tax year 2027), that
                is 30 September 2027, unless the FBR extends it.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">How to file</h3>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Log in to FBR IRIS.</li>
                <li>Open the income tax return for the right tax year.</li>
                <li>
                    Enter your foreign export income in the final or fixed tax
                    section for export of IT services, not in ordinary business
                    income. Putting it in the wrong place can push the whole
                    amount into the normal slabs.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Enter the total export receipts and the tax already deducted
                    by the bank.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Complete your wealth statement and reconcile it with your
                    remittances.
                </li>
                <li>Submit before the deadline.</li>
            </ol>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                What if I file late?
            </h3>
            <p>
                You can lose ATL status. That triggers higher withholding on
                banking and other transactions and puts your final-tax treatment
                at risk. Do not skip the filing, even if your tax was already
                deducted.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Is There Tax on Withdrawing Rs 50,000?
            </h2>
            <p>
                This confuses many freelancers, because it is a different tax
                from the export tax.
            </p>
            <p>Section 231AB applies to cash withdrawals from banks:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    <strong>Filers (on ATL):</strong> no withholding on cash withdrawals.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Non-filers:</strong> 0.8% on cash withdrawn above Rs 50,000
                    in a day, charged on the whole amount.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>So:</strong></p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Withdrawing exactly Rs 50,000: no tax, because the threshold
                    applies only above Rs 50,000.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Withdrawing Rs 50,500 as a non-filer: about Rs 404 (0.8%).
                </li>
                <li>
                    Withdrawing any amount as a filer: no tax under this
                    section.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Two clarifications:</strong></p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    This tax applies to cash withdrawals only, not to receiving
                    money from abroad. Inward remittances are not hit by this
                    rule.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Moving money by online transfer is not a "cash withdrawal"
                    for this purpose.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Another reason to become a filer: it also removes this deduction
                from your life.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Common Mistakes That Cost Freelancers Money
            </h2>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    <strong>Not registering with PSEB.</strong> You pay four times
                    as much tax.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Letting PSEB registration lapse.</strong> The bank may
                    deduct 1% until you renew.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Not filing a return.</strong> You can lose ATL status
                    and final-tax protection.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Using informal channels.</strong> Crypto, hawala or third-party
                    transfers can't be documented as export income and can raise questions
                    about unexplained funds.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Entering export income in the wrong tab.</strong> This
                    can expose the whole amount to slab rates.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Not checking the bank's deduction.</strong> Banks sometimes
                    deduct 1% even after you give them your PSEB certificate. Check
                    every credit.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Mixing local and foreign income.</strong> Keep two sets
                    of records.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Claiming expenses against export tax.</strong> Under the
                    final tax regime, you can't.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <strong>What if my bank deducted too much?</strong> Gather your PSEB
                certificate and the remittance advice, and write to the bank to ask
                for a correction. If it is not fixed, the excess may be claimed as
                a credit when you file, but cash refunds can be slow. Catching it
                early is much easier.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Freelancer Tax Calculator Pakistan: How to Use One
            </h2>
            <p>A good freelancer tax calculator needs five inputs:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Monthly or annual income in USD or PKR</li>
                <li>The exchange rate on the credit date</li>
                <li>PSEB status (registered or not)</li>
                <li>Filer status (on ATL or not)</li>
                <li>Whether the income is from foreign or local clients</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                It will then show monthly and annual tax and your net income.
                Remember that calculators give estimates. Your bank's actual
                deduction depends on its records and the rate on the day.
            </p>
            <p>
                For local-client income or a salaried job on the side, use our{
                    " "
                }
                <a
                    href="/blog/salary-tax-calculator-pakistan/"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                >
                    salary tax calculator
                </a>{" "}
                and combine the results.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>For context: salaried slabs from 1 July 2026</strong>
            </p>
            <p>
                Under the Finance Act 2026, salaried individuals pay no tax up
                to Rs 600,000 and 1% on income from Rs 600,000 to Rs 1.2
                million. The top slab, above Rs 7 million, is Rs 1,424,000 plus
                35% of the excess. Non-salaried individuals are taxed on a
                separate schedule with a higher top rate, so check the current
                schedule if you have large local income.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Frequently Asked Questions
            </h2>
            <div class="space-y-4 mt-3">
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Do I need to pay tax if I am a freelancer in Pakistan?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Yes. Freelance income is taxable. Foreign income for IT
                        and IT-enabled services is taxed at a low export rate,
                        usually deducted by your bank. Local income is taxed
                        under normal rules. You should also file an annual
                        return.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        How much tax will I pay as a freelancer?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        A PSEB-registered filer pays 0.25% on export proceeds,
                        about Rs 2,500 per Rs 1 million. Without PSEB, a filer
                        pays 1%, about Rs 10,000 per Rs 1 million.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        What are the freelancer tax rates in Pakistan in 2026?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        For FY 2026-27: 0.25% with PSEB registration and 1%
                        without it, for filers on the ATL. Non-filers face
                        higher deductions — confirm with your bank. The 0.25%
                        concession is extended through tax year 2029.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Is there a tax on a withdrawal of 50,000 rupees?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Not if you are a filer. Non-filers pay 0.8% only on cash
                        withdrawals above Rs 50,000 in a day. Exactly Rs 50,000
                        is not taxed.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Is PSEB registration mandatory?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        No, but without it you pay 1% instead of 0.25%.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Is the 0.25% tax final?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Yes, if you meet the Section 154A conditions: PSEB
                        registration, ATL status, bank channels and filing your
                        return.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Can I deduct expenses?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        No. The tax applies at a fixed rate and expenses do not
                        reduce it.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        What if I earn from both foreign and local clients?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Foreign income follows the export rules. Local income is
                        business income under normal rules. Keep them separate.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Final Thoughts</h2>
            <p>
                Freelancing tax in Pakistan is one of the lightest regimes
                available if you follow the rules. The system is simple:
                register with PSEB, get on the ATL, receive money through your
                bank, and file your return every year. Do that, and you will
                typically pay 0.25% on foreign IT income. Skip it, and you pay
                four times as much, or worse.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Your action plan:</strong></p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Get your NTN and join the ATL.</li>
                <li>Register with PSEB and note the renewal date.</li>
                <li>Tell your bank, and check every remittance deduction.</li>
                <li>Keep remittance advices and invoices together.</li>
                <li>File your return before 30 September each year.</li>
                <li>
                    Estimate any salary or local-client income with our{" "}
